(DRAFT#32)(IV)(EE)(204)&emsp;Let's assume for a moment that Mr. Le Faucheur did indeed discuss me privately with McDonald's executives, including perhaps Mr. Penichon. What would that conversation look like given the incriminating evidence I had presented?

(DRAFT#32)(IV)(EE)(205)&emsp;Given the magnitude of this case — as it pertains to the economic security of the United States, France, and other nations — a thorough investigation is warranted.

(DRAFT#32)(IV)(EE)(206)&emsp;In the wake of these events, I eventually lodged a criminal complaint in France, believing that the French judicial system would uphold justice.

(DRAFT#32)(IV)(EE)(207)&emsp;The ensuing manipulation and alteration of public and legal documents in this case is a topic I will address in subsequent sections. Refer to the screenshot provided below for verification.

(DRAFT#32)(IV)(EE)(208)&emsp;**Aggravating circumstance number 10 for the 2016 frauds in France:**

(DRAFT#32)(IV)(EE)(209)&emsp;On January 27, 2017, at 20:39 (China Standard Time), I submitted a criminal complaint for defamation to the Paris Public Prosecutor's Office.

(DRAFT#32)(IV)(EE)(210)&emsp;Paris Public Prosecutor François Molins responded directly to me, noting: "Well received on 30/01/2017."

(DRAFT#32)(IV)(EE)(211)&emsp;[DRAFT NOTE: Continue with the rest of the narrative regarding the complaint and subsequent events]

(DRAFT#32)(IV)(EE)(212)&emsp;Given that my submission was a criminal complaint for defamation — which is a private prosecution matter in France — the direct involvement of the Paris Public Prosecutor was unexpected.

(DRAFT#32)(IV)(EE)(213)&emsp;Yet, an astonishing development occurred: I received an email from the Paris Public Prosecutor's Office dated February 2, 2017, stating that my complaint had been registered on January 30, 2017.

(DRAFT#32)(IV)(EE)(214)&emsp;This revelation left me bewildered. After all, François Molins, the Paris Public Prosecutor, had personally acknowledged receipt on January 30, 2017.

(DRAFT#32)(IV)(EE)(215)&emsp;The glaring discrepancy in these dates leads to one unsettling conclusion: a potential manipulation of official records.

(DRAFT#32)(IV)(EE)(216)&emsp;[DRAFT NOTE: Continue elaborating on document manipulation]

(DRAFT#32)(IV)(EE)(217)&emsp;I had previously lodged a criminal complaint with the Paris Public Prosecutor's Office concerning the broader frauds perpetrated by McDonald's.

(DRAFT#32)(IV)(EE)(218)&emsp;In my initial complaint, I cautioned the Paris Public Prosecutor, stating: "Beware, the case involves multinational corporations with extensive resources and influence."

(DRAFT#32)(IV)(EE)(219)&emsp;Based on the definition provided by French law professor Yves Strickler, the term "faux" (forgery) in French law encompasses any fraudulent alteration of the truth in written documents that could result in prejudice.

(DRAFT#32)(IV)(EE)(220)&emsp;Personally, I've encountered between 20 to 50 instances, if not more, of such document alterations in various legal proceedings. While this figure may seem staggering, it underscores the systemic nature of this issue.

(DRAFT#32)(IV)(EE)(221)&emsp;These incidents were not solely related to the McDonald's case. From my observations, they appear to be symptomatic of a broader dysfunction within the French judicial system.

(DRAFT#32)(IV)(EE)(222)&emsp;While it's concerning to witness such shortcuts and manipulations, which fundamentally undermine the rule of law, one must question whether this is an isolated phenomenon or indicative of systemic corruption.

(DRAFT#32)(IV)(EE)(223)&emsp;The criminal activities orchestrated by McDonald's Corporation have inflicted immeasurable harm on countless individuals across multiple jurisdictions.

(DRAFT#32)(IV)(EE)(224)&emsp;In hindsight, during 2015 and 2017, I was unaware of the special relationship between McDonald's legal representatives and high-ranking French officials.

(DRAFT#32)(IV)(EE)(225)&emsp;The law firm Péchenard & Associés, hired by McDonald's France, was established by the parents of Frédéric Péchenard, the former General Director of the French National Police.

(DRAFT#32)(IV)(EE)(226)&emsp;Furthermore, communication sent to me by Éric Andrieu also bears the name of Chantal PÉCHENARD, Frédéric's mother, on the letterhead.

(DRAFT#32)(IV)(EE)(227)&emsp;Adding to the complexity, McDonald's Corporation's global compliance rules explicitly prohibit conflicts of interest. Yet, their choice of legal representation in France appears to flagrantly violate these very rules.

(DRAFT#32)(IV)(EE)(228)&emsp;Decades ago, McDonald's France faced scrutiny from the French authorities for conducting unlawful lotteries.

(DRAFT#32)(IV)(EE)(229)&emsp;Yet, in recent times, despite ample evidence proving that McDonald's France has engaged in similar if not more egregious conduct, no meaningful action has been taken.

(DRAFT#32)(IV)(EE)(230)&emsp;This led me to write an open letter to François Molins, which was later published on my website.

(DRAFT#32)(IV)(EE)(231)&emsp;Below, I provide a succinct summary translated into English: [DRAFT NOTE: Insert translation of open letter summary]

(DRAFT#32)(IV)(EE)(232)&emsp;I explicitly told French top Public Prosecutor Mr. Molins that "Having said this, I firmly believe that you and your colleagues are honest public servants doing their best under difficult circumstances."

(DRAFT#32)(IV)(EE)(233)&emsp;Now more than ever, I stand by this statement. Additionally, it's worth noting that in subsequent communications, I extended the benefit of the doubt to Mr. Molins.

(DRAFT#32)(IV)(EE)(234)&emsp;However, I am extending the benefit of the doubt to Mr. Molins and choosing to believe that any irregularities were not of his doing but rather the result of subordinates acting without his knowledge or approval.

